          "The four reported demographic categories sum to approximately 95.7%, leaving a ~4.3% gap that in Mississippi most likely represents American Indian or Alaska Native residents, though it may also reflect mixed-race or unclassified respondents — worth noting but not overstating as a swing constituency at this scale. The more politically significant demographic is the 14.7% Black community, which in a district carried without opposition at 100% Republican is almost certainly either undervoting or strategically disengaged rather than genuinely represented. At 39.9% renter rate and $46,296 income, economic stress is the shared material condition cutting across racial lines in this district: renters facing housing cost pressure and working-age households without college credentials navigating a constrained labor market are constituencies whose needs, if unaddressed, create the conditions for a credible challenger to emerge and make an unopposed seat competitive."
