          { name: "Full-District Constituent Service Including the Black Community", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 8.01% Black population in a district that went 80-20 Republican almost certainly represents the near-totality of the 20% Democratic vote — meaning Black residents are voting at high rates against the incumbent while remaining constituents entitled to full casework, infrastructure advocacy, and legislative attention. A representative who delivers responsive service across racial lines in an 80-20 district is not taking political risk — they are building the kind of community reputation that makes challengers implausible and prevents the 20% from becoming an organizing base for a future competitive cycle. Ignoring the 20% because they are electorally non-threatening is the strategic error that turns safe seats into embarrassments." },
          { name: "Workforce Development & Education Pipeline", tag: "careful", why: "At 17.6% college attainment, MS-HD-3 has a constituency that is broadly skeptical of four-year college as the universal economic pathway — and messaging that centers college education as the solution to economic mobility will land poorly with the 82% of adults who did not follow that path. The strategic opportunity is vocational and technical workforce development: credential programs, community college pathways, and employer partnerships that create local economic mobility without requiring relocation to a university town. This requires careful framing — emphasizing trades, local employment, and keeping young people in the community — rather than generic education rhetoric that can read as condescending to non-college households." },
          { name: "Treating 80% as a Mandate for Neglect", tag: "avoid", why: "An 80% margin creates a specific psychological trap for incumbents: the assumption that the seat is structurally safe regardless of performance removes the accountability mechanism that drives responsive governance. In Mississippi's rural districts at this income level, constituent frustration with absentee representation has demonstrated the capacity to generate primary challengers who consolidate the working-class base around a 'more present' alternative. The 80% result reflects consolidated partisan preference, not personal loyalty — and personal loyalty is what distinguishes a durable incumbent from a placeholder who loses a primary they never saw coming." },
          { name: "Abstract Culture-War Positioning as a Substitute for Economic Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "A $50,584 median income, 17.6% college attainment constituency is not well-served by a legislative record dominated by abstract ideological signaling at the expense of concrete economic and infrastructure outcomes. Working-class rural households in Mississippi evaluate their representative on tangible results — is the road fixed, is the hospital still open, did the economic development project come through — and a representative whose primary visibility is culture-war positioning rather than service delivery will eventually find that the 80% coalition contains a significant bloc of economically frustrated voters who will support a primary challenger promising to actually govern. The 80-20 margin is maintained by economic delivery to the base, not by ideological performance for an audience outside the district." }
