          { name: "Black Community Constituent Service and Policy Engagement", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 21.37%, the Black community in MS-HD-10 is too large to govern around — it represents more than one in five constituents, and in any contested race, its mobilization level determines whether the seat is comfortable or competitive. The current uncontested dynamic conceals rather than resolves this strategic reality: an opponent who makes a credible case that the district's Black residents are underserved can convert passive non-participation into active opposition. Genuine constituent engagement — specific attention to economic mobility, healthcare access, and educational opportunity for Black households — is both a governance obligation and the single most effective insurance policy against a competitive challenge. At $69,434 median income, not all economic stress in this district falls equally across racial lines, and a legislator who acknowledges that directly builds trust that outlasts any single election cycle." },
          { name: "Renter Community and Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "The 26.2% renter rate in a district with $69,434 median income is a signal of economic stratification — renters in a relatively prosperous district often face the sharpest cost-burden dynamics, and this population likely overlaps significantly with the Black and younger adult communities. Ignoring renter concerns in favor of exclusive homeowner messaging risks alienating a constituency that, if mobilized by a future challenger, represents a meaningful share of potential swing voters. However, housing policy in Mississippi legislative races carries local political complexity — aggressive affordability framing can create friction with the homeowner majority if perceived as threatening property values or neighborhood character. The strategic posture is attentive constituent service and targeted communication rather than housing as a lead campaign issue." },
          { name: "Treating the Uncontested Win as Proof of Broad Support", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% result from an uncontested race conveys zero information about voter satisfaction, incumbent approval, or coalition depth — it records only that no opposing candidate qualified. MS-HD-10's demographic composition, with a 21% Black community, a 26% renter population, and meaningful Hispanic and Asian minorities, means the current political stability rests on electoral circumstances rather than measured constituent alignment. A campaign or governing posture that treats the 2023 result as reflecting genuine supermajority support will under-invest in constituent relationships and fail to identify vulnerabilities before an opponent does. The most dangerous assumption in an uncontested district is that the absence of a challenger means the absence of dissatisfaction." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Hispanic and Asian Communities as Electorally Irrelevant", tag: "avoid", why: "At 3.22% Hispanic and 1.01% Asian, these communities are small in current percentage terms — but Mississippi's demographic trajectory shows steady growth in both populations, and early relational investment in these communities pays compounding electoral returns as they grow. More immediately, in a district where the margin of safety depends on broad non-mobilization of non-white constituencies, any community that feels categorically ignored by their representative becomes a potential organizing node for a future challenger. Constituent service visibility — showing up, responding, and legislating in ways that acknowledge their presence — costs little and eliminates a potential vulnerability before it becomes one." }
