          { name: "Black Community Institutional Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "With 62.57% Black majority, this district's primary constituency has specific institutional relationships — churches, HBCUs, civil rights organizations, community development financial institutions — that function as trust networks and political information channels. Engagement that routes through these institutions with genuine reciprocity is essential; engagement that treats them as voter-mobilization infrastructure without substantive policy follow-through will be recognized and remembered. The careful dimension is ensuring that community engagement is substantive and two-way rather than performative, particularly given that an unopposed result can breed the complacency of assuming community support without continuously earning it." },
          { name: "Running on Partisan Identity Rather Than Constituent Delivery", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district that has produced a 100% unopposed Democratic result, there may be a temptation to treat partisan alignment as the primary electoral asset — but that conflates the absence of Republican competition with affirmative constituent enthusiasm. At $36,380 income with 40.3% renters and 13.3% college attainment, this constituency evaluates their representative on whether conditions are improving, not on whether they carry the right party label. A campaign or governing posture that leads with partisan framing rather than specific economic delivery records will hollow out the incumbent's legitimacy precisely among the most economically stressed constituents who have the least reason to engage with abstract partisan politics." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Status as a Governing Mandate", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% result with no opponent is electorally comfortable but analytically empty — it reveals nothing about constituent satisfaction, engagement levels, or whether the district's needs are being met. In Mississippi's low-turnout legislative election environment, an unopposed result can mask a deeply disengaged electorate rather than a deeply satisfied one. The district's economic indicators — near-poverty median income, low educational attainment, high renter vulnerability — suggest that material conditions provide abundant grounds for primary challenge or organized disengagement if a credible alternative emerges. Governing as though the seat is permanently safe is the behavior most likely to make it competitive." }
