          { name: "Minority Community Engagement Before Competition Forces It", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 18.12% Black and 9.05% Hispanic populations together constitute more than 27% of MS-HD-15 — a combined minority bloc large enough to determine the outcome of any competitive race. Because Waldo ran unopposed in 2023, there is no data on how these communities would vote in a contested election, which means any incumbent who assumes their support has made an assumption without evidence. In Mississippi, Black voter mobilization in particular has repeatedly demonstrated the capacity to reshape district-level outcomes when organized around specific economic and healthcare concerns. Proactive engagement — through community institutions, churches, and local organizations — before a challenger can establish those relationships first is the only defensible strategic posture for a seat where 27% of the population has never been asked to choose." },
          { name: "Renter and Housing Affordability", tag: "careful", why: "The 34.1% renter rate is the most structurally important economic signal in the district after median income, and it cuts across the racial composition of the constituency — renters in a $53,616-income district face real housing cost pressure regardless of race. However, housing policy in Mississippi legislative races requires careful framing: landlord and property-owner interests are often better-organized politically than renter constituencies, and a district that is still 65.9% homeowner contains a constituency that can perceive renter-focused policy as a threat to property values or a signal of urban-style governance priorities. The strategic path is to frame housing affordability as a working-family economic security issue — keeping families stable and rooted in the community — rather than a tenant-rights framework that may alienate the homeowner majority." },
          { name: "Treating the Unopposed Win as a Measure of Constituent Satisfaction", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% partisan result from an unopposed race reveals nothing about how the district would vote in a competitive election — it is an absence of data, not a confirmation of strength. An incumbent who campaigns future cycles on the basis of 2023's uncontested result is strategically blind to the district's actual preferences, particularly among the 27% minority population and the 34.1% renter bloc. In Mississippi, districts that appear structurally safe have repeatedly produced surprise competitive results when a credible challenger organizes constituencies that an incumbent has taken for granted. The 2023 result should be treated as a warning about unknown vulnerability, not a reassurance of electoral security." },
          { name: "Ignoring the Low College-Attainment Majority's Skepticism of Abstract Policy Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "At 16.9% college attainment, MS-HD-15 has one of the lowest educational attainment profiles a district can carry, and messaging calibrated for college-educated audiences — abstract policy frameworks, credentialed expertise appeals, or ideologically coded language — will systematically fail to land with the district's majority. Working-class voters at this income and education level evaluate candidates on perceived understanding of their daily economic circumstances, not on policy sophistication. A candidate whose communication style, vocabulary, or campaign materials read as designed for a different audience will lose the trust of the majority before making any substantive argument, and no amount of correct positioning on the issues will recover that basic credibility failure." }
