          { name: "Cross-Racial Constituent Service", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 15.58% Black population in a Mississippi district is not a passive demographic — it is a constituency with deep civic infrastructure, high organizational capacity, and the demonstrated ability to mobilize rapidly when motivated by a credible candidate or grievance. In a district where the current margin is 100% only because no one ran, the Black community's assessment of the incumbent's responsiveness is the single most consequential variable in whether that changes. Visible constituent service — attending community events, responding to casework, showing up at Black churches and civic organizations without an election imminent — is not coalition-building theater; it is the concrete record that makes a challenger's mobilization pitch harder to land." },
          { name: "Public Education Funding and Quality", tag: "careful", why: "With 30.3% college attainment and a median age of 39.0, the district contains a meaningful professional class alongside working-class families for whom public school quality is a direct household concern — but education funding in Mississippi carries racial and economic equity dimensions that require careful navigation. Any framing that sounds like opposition to public school investment will land poorly with the 15.58% Black constituency and with the college-educated white households who depend on district school quality for property values. This issue is a genuine governing priority for the district but must be handled with awareness that education rhetoric in Mississippi carries historical weight that extends well beyond budgetary technicalities." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed as Permanently Safe", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% result from an unopposed race is not a mandate — it is an absence of opposition, and those are strategically distinct. In Mississippi's political environment, a well-resourced challenger who activates the Black community (15.58%), runs up margins among college-educated voters frustrated by specific governance failures, and ties together a coalition around healthcare and school quality could turn this into a 55-45 race before the incumbent's campaign infrastructure is even assembled. The complacency risk is not theoretical; it is what the unopposed result structurally produces. Any incumbent who treats the 2023 result as evidence of broad popularity rather than organizational vacuum is misreading the data." },
          { name: "Racially Coded Wedge Messaging", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district that is 75.94% white and 15.58% Black in Mississippi — a state with the deepest racial political history in the country — any campaign messaging that relies on racially coded appeals to consolidate the white majority will produce short-term wins at the cost of long-term governing capacity and constituent trust. At $77,118 income, the district's white homeowners share many material concerns with the Black community around healthcare, schools, and economic development; messaging that divides these constituencies along racial lines forecloses the governing coalitions that actually deliver on those shared concerns. More practically: a representative whose record is perceived as racially exclusionary is handing any future challenger the most powerful mobilizing narrative available in this state." }
