          { name: "Housing Stability & Renter Protections", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 31.7% renter rate in a district with a $47,160 median income means a substantial portion of the constituency is spending significant fractions of household income on housing with little protection against market disruption, substandard conditions, or displacement. Renters at this income level have minimal financial cushion — a rent spike or habitability crisis is a genuine household emergency, not an inconvenience. Legislative focus on affordable housing investment, tenant protections, and housing assistance programs addresses the district's most economically exposed constituency directly and visibly, producing the kind of concrete constituent benefit that sustains the trust behind an uncontested result." },
          { name: "Multiracial Constituency Engagement", tag: "careful", why: "With 25.36% white and 7.08% Hispanic residents alongside the 63.58% Black majority, HD-27 is not a monoracial constituency even if its electoral base is strongly consolidated around a single community. The white and Hispanic communities have their own economic and service concerns — workforce access, healthcare, and community investment — that require specific constituent attention rather than passive assumption of alignment with the majority's priorities. In an uncontested seat, there is no electoral pressure to broaden outreach, which creates the risk that secondary constituencies become invisible to the representative and eventually disconnected from the political process entirely, reducing both turnout and the breadth of the mandate." },
          { name: "Treating Uncontested Status as Proof of Satisfaction", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% uncontested result tells you nothing about constituent satisfaction — it tells you no one filed to run against the incumbent, which in a safe majority-minority district often reflects structural barriers to opposition candidacy rather than universal approval. At $47,160 income and 14.6% college attainment, the district's working-class constituency has specific unmet economic needs, and a representative who interprets the absence of competition as evidence that those needs are adequately addressed will find that conclusion challenged when turnout softens or a credible challenger eventually emerges. Complacency is the specific vulnerability of the uncontested seat." },
          { name: "Abstract or Ideological Messaging Disconnected from Material Needs", tag: "avoid", why: "In a district where the median household earns $47,160, fewer than 1 in 6 adults holds a college degree, and nearly a third of residents rent their homes, the constituency evaluates representation in terms of material outcomes — not ideological positioning or legislative symbolism. Messaging that foregrounds partisan identity, national political narratives, or abstract policy frameworks without connecting them to concrete local economic outcomes will fail to resonate with a working-class majority-Black constituency whose daily concerns are income, housing, healthcare access, and workforce opportunity. The district's demographics demand a representative whose communication is grounded in the specific economic realities of HD-27." }
