          { name: "Black Community Constituent Service and Economic Representation", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 28.72% Black population is large enough to determine electoral outcomes if mobilized around an alternative candidate, yet may be invisible in an incumbent's constituent service and legislative priorities if they default to geographic or demographic blindness. In a district where median income is $60,361 and economic security is the primary governing concern, the Black community's specific economic challenges — employment discrimination, healthcare access, housing discrimination, educational opportunity — deserve active legislative attention and constituent service, not token acknowledgment. An incumbent who can point to concrete legislative record and ongoing constituent engagement on these issues builds genuine loyalty; an incumbent who is invisible to this population creates an opening for organized opposition." },
          { name: "Unopposed 2023 as a Vulnerability Signal Rather Than a Strength Signal", tag: "careful", why: "An unopposed election result can reflect either genuine constituent loyalty or simply the absence of an organized challenger in that particular cycle — these are strategically opposite conclusions about the same electoral fact. In MS-HD-33, understanding which explanation is true requires the incumbent to conduct active diagnostic work: Are constituents at $60K income reporting improved economic circumstances, better healthcare access, more affordable housing? Are they aware of the incumbent's legislative record on these issues? Do they feel represented and served? If the answers are affirmative, the 2023 unopposed result reflects genuine strength; if they are negative or uncertain, the incumbent is vulnerable to any challenger who articulates a credible economic platform and invests in constituent engagement." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed 2023 as Evidence of Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "One unopposed cycle does not create a safe seat — it creates a district where no viable challenger emerged in that particular cycle, which is a different strategic fact entirely. In a working-class constituency at $60K median income under economic pressure, voters are highly responsive to quality-of-life improvements and legislative delivery on economic concerns; they are not voting based on inherited partisan loyalty or previous cycle margins. Any incumbent who uses an unopposed result as justification for reduced constituent engagement or legislative attention is actively undermining the conditions that produced that unopposed outcome." },
          { name: "Ignoring the 33.7% Renter Constituency's Housing and Economic Stability Concerns", tag: "avoid", why: "At 33.7% renters in a district with $60,361 median income, housing affordability and rental market stability are direct economic concerns for one-third of the constituency — yet these issues often receive inadequate legislative attention in districts where homeowners are politically organized and vocal. Renter households at this income level face specific vulnerabilities: rising rental costs consuming increasing shares of income, vulnerability to displacement if market rents rise faster than wages, lack of wealth-building through homeownership. An incumbent who neglects renter-specific economic policy in favor of homeowner-focused initiatives is ignoring one-third of the constituency and creating an opening for opposition mobilization around housing justice and economic fairness." }
