          { name: "Homeowner Economic Security & Cost-of-Living Pressure", tag: "lean-into", why: "With an 81.2% homeownership rate and $73,331 median household income, MS-HD-37 is a community of property owners whose material concerns center on property tax stability, insurance costs, and housing affordability as those costs rise. The 37.3-year median age and 18.8% renter rate indicate a relatively stable, asset-holding constituency for whom local governance on tax policy, municipal services, and infrastructure maintenance directly affects household finances. An incumbent who can demonstrate concrete delivery on property-owner concerns—or a challenger who can credibly promise better stewardship of these issues—finds a responsive constituency. The unopposed margin suggests the current representative has not faced pressure to demonstrate this capacity; a competitive challenger could exploit that gap." },
          { name: "Low College Attainment & Workforce Development", tag: "careful", why: "At 26% college-educated adults, MS-HD-37 has the lowest college attainment in the five-district sequence and reflects a working-class constituency whose economic mobility depends on access to skilled trades, workforce development, and pathways to stable employment outside four-year degree programs. Community college funding, apprenticeship programs, and vocational education infrastructure are not abstract policy issues but direct economic concerns for families in a district where three-quarters of adults lack a college degree. An incumbent or challenger who can speak with specificity about workforce pathways, rather than defaulting to generalized education rhetoric, demonstrates understanding of what actually constrains economic opportunity in this community. The unopposed victory provides no data on whether the current representative engages this constituency with that level of specificity." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as Evidence of Genuine Constituent Satisfaction", tag: "avoid", why: "Boyd won unopposed in 2023, which reveals nothing about actual constituent preferences, satisfaction levels, or the intensity of support across the district's four major demographic groups—70.61% white, 25.14% Black, 1.47% Hispanic, and 0.71% Asian. An unopposed victory is consistent with an incumbent who is genuinely serving the district well and also consistent with an incumbent facing a disorganized or discouraged opposition, low voter interest in a non-competitive race, or a constituency that is politically dormant rather than actively engaged. Any strategy that treats the 100% result as evidence that the current representation is secure is mathematically wrong; the absence of a challenger is not the same as evidence of universal satisfaction, and a credible challenger with concrete economic messaging could shift the dynamics significantly." },
          { name: "Neglecting the Hispanic & Asian Communities Despite Small Numbers", tag: "avoid", why: "At 1.47% Hispanic and 0.71% Asian, these communities represent only about 2.2% of the district's reported population—small enough that a representative focused exclusively on the white and Black constituencies could rationalize not engaging them. However, in a district with 81.2% homeownership and $73,331 income, even a small Hispanic community likely includes both agricultural workers and small business owners with concrete stakes in water policy, labor law, and economic development. Any incumbent or challenger who consciously excludes these constituencies from outreach—even because they are numerically small—risks both alienating them and missing opportunities to build durable support among groups whose economic concerns and civic engagement may be underestimated. The unopposed victory provides no data on whether these communities feel represented." }
