          "The 2023 unopposed result is a strategic asset for the current representative but should not create complacency about the district's fundamentals. In a majority-Black urban district with median income below the state average and no Republican opposition currently fielded, the relevant electoral competition is Democratic primary dynamics, potential migration of working-class voters to neighboring districts with lower rents and job opportunities, and turnout variation in non-presidential years. The 18.7% college attainment rate — below the state and national average — signals a constituency less engaged with higher education as a pathway and more directly dependent on local economic conditions, service quality, and whether government is visibly delivering for working families. A representative who consolidates core Black voter support through consistent district presence, addresses the material economic concerns of renters and low-income homeowners, and builds relationships with neighborhood institutions — churches, nonprofits, local businesses — is building the durable constituency that makes a 100% result in an unopposed race the floor, not the ceiling, for future competitive elections."
