          { name: "Education Quality and School Funding", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 46% college-educated adults and $96,042 median household income, MS-HD-59's constituency has both the education level and economic resources to be sophisticated consumers of education policy. Schools are likely the largest local public investment and a core quality-of-life determinant for this affluent, family-oriented community. A representative who articulates a specific vision for how the state should fund schools competitively, attract and retain excellent teachers, and position the district's students for college and professional success is speaking directly to what this constituency values and evaluates on materially." },
          { name: "Property Tax Fairness and Fiscal Sustainability", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 83.7% homeownership and median household income at $96K, property owners in MS-HD-59 are directly exposed to property tax burdens and the quality of public services those taxes fund. At this income level and homeownership rate, households are sensitive to the total cost equation — both the explicit tax burden and the quality of infrastructure, schools, and public safety they receive in return. A representative who engages specifically with property tax fairness, the sustainability of local government funding, and the state's role in preventing tax burden growth is addressing a core economic security concern for the district's majority." },
          { name: "Understanding the Unopposed 2023 Victory", tag: "careful", why: "Powell's unopposed 2023 victory is unusual and requires strategic investigation to understand whether it reflects genuine incumbent strength or organizational weakness among potential challengers. If unopposed status is the result of weak opposition infrastructure rather than constituent satisfaction with Powell's service on material deliverables, it does not constitute a durable electoral advantage. Any candidate — incumbent or challenger — should treat this seat as genuinely competitive if opposition emerges with a coherent message about what the district is not receiving, rather than assuming the 2023 result is predictive of future elections in a high-expectations constituency." },
          { name: "Partisan Identity as the Primary Electoral Driver", tag: "avoid", why: "MS-HD-59's affluent, educated, homeowner-dominant constituency evaluates representatives primarily on concrete delivery of public goods and fiscal management — not on partisan rhetoric or ideological positioning. A campaign that defaults to partisan messaging rather than specific proposals about education funding, infrastructure quality, property tax fairness, and economic competitiveness will underperform with a constituency that has high expectations for governmental competence and material results. This district will not support a candidate based on partisan loyalty alone; it will support one who credibly commits to delivering on the issues that affect daily quality of life." },
          { name: "Treating 46% College Education as Passive Support", tag: "avoid", why: "The 46% college-educated adult population is not a passive constituency — it is a group with active engagement in policy issues, high expectations for substantive debate, and low tolerance for oversimplification or evasion. A candidate who attempts to manage this constituency with simplified messaging, avoidance of complex policy questions, or dismissal of their concerns will find that education level translates into active skepticism rather than passive agreement. This is a constituency that reads, questions, and evaluates — and expects their representative to engage at the same level of seriousness they bring to their own decision-making." }
