          { name: "Housing Affordability & Renter Stability", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 48% renter rate and $45,546 median household income, nearly half the district's households are exposed to rent increases that directly compress their ability to afford food, healthcare, transportation, and other essentials. Rent-to-income ratios in this income band typically exceed 40-50% of gross income, creating acute financial stress. State-level policy on rental assistance, tenant protections, and affordable housing development are high-impact legislative areas where a representative can materially improve district conditions and demonstrate concrete constituent service. Housing stability is the material prerequisite for economic mobility and family security in a working-poor constituency." },
          { name: "Economic Mobility & Workforce Development", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 24.3% college attainment rate — substantially below Mississippi's state average — and the young 36.8 median age together indicate a constituency where pathways to higher-wage employment are constrained by education access and job-market opportunity. Community college affordability, vocational training, apprenticeship access, and state-supported job placement programs are legislative priorities that directly address why young adults in MS-HD-70 face economic constraints. A representative who champions workforce development policy and connects district residents to training and employment opportunities builds the constituent relationship that durability in supermajority seats requires." },
          { name: "Immigrant Community Economic & Language Access", tag: "careful", why: "The 1.51% Hispanic and 2.31% Asian populations represent ~3.8% of the district and are often invisible in political analysis of supermajority Black seats, but they are present households with specific economic and social needs. Employment discrimination, housing discrimination, language barriers to accessing services, and immigration-status anxiety are material concerns for immigrant and immigrant-connected households. These communities require direct constituent attention and specific policy advocacy — around workplace rights, access to services, and protection from discrimination — rather than being absorbed into generic minority-community approaches. Ignoring them risks both failing to represent constituents and allowing a competing political force to build influence in a growing demographic." },
          { name: "Treating Supermajority Status as Permanent Electoral Safety", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2023 result is a current political fact, not a structural guarantee. If constituent service declines, if the district's material conditions deteriorate (housing costs rise, job quality declines, public services degrade) without visible legislative response, or if turnout infrastructure atrophies, the supermajority can shift rapidly. Supermajority seats are durable only through active maintenance: continuous constituent engagement, measurable policy delivery, and genuine relationship-building with community leadership. A representative who treats MS-HD-70 as automatically safe and diverts organizational energy to influence-building elsewhere will find that assumption tested when a credible challenger emerges." },
          { name: "Misaligning Legislative Priorities With District Material Needs", tag: "avoid", why: "A constituency at $45,546 median income with 48% renting and 24.3% college attainment evaluates representation on whether government materially improves their lives — not on ideological positioning or abstract political narratives. If a representative prioritizes legislative agendas that do not translate into housing stability, job access, education quality, or healthcare access for district residents, the supermajority can become a liability rather than an asset. The constituency's economic anxiety is real and present; legislative priorities must reflect that reality or the representative risks being perceived as disconnected from actual constituent concerns." }
