          { name: "Affluent Suburban Stability & Property Wealth Protection", tag: "lean-into", why: "MS-HD-74 is a high-income ($94,932 median), college-educated (44.4%), predominantly white (75.25%) suburban district where 80.4% are homeowners — the profile of a constituency with substantial accumulated property wealth and a direct stake in tax policy, school funding mechanisms, and neighborhood stability. At these income and education levels, property tax burden, education quality, and residential amenity preservation are the material concerns that drive voter behavior. An unopposed 2023 win indicates current representation is consolidated, but any challenger or successor must lead with the specific tax and education policies that protect and enhance the property value and quality-of-life premium that existing residents have secured." },
          { name: "College-Educated Professional Constituency & Higher Education Pathway", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 44.4% college attainment — among the highest in the state — and a median age of 42.8, MS-HD-74 is a district of educated professionals and families who have navigated higher education and are invested in education quality downstream. University affordability, K-12 curriculum rigor, and whether public education is delivering for professional-class families are not abstract policy questions but direct household concerns affecting children's competitive positioning. This constituency evaluates on whether government is maintaining the educational and economic infrastructure that justified their own educational investment and their current residential choice." },
          { name: "Silent Demographic: Understanding the 3.6% Unaccounted Population", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported demographic categories account for 96.48% of HD-74's population, leaving a ~3.6% gap — smaller than other districts but non-trivial in a 19.6% renter population where demographic transition may be occurring. In an affluent suburban district, this gap may represent recent immigrant professional families, international students, or transient populations not yet fully counted in Census data. At $94,932 income and 44.4% college education, any growth in this population likely reflects professional-class diversity rather than economic vulnerability, but a legislator who remains aware of demographic evolution in an affluent district is better positioned to recognize and respond to constituency shifts." },
          { name: "Treating an Unopposed Win as Durable Political Consolidation", tag: "avoid", why: "The 2023 unopposed result (100% Rep, 0% Dem) may reflect genuine district alignment, but it may equally reflect Democratic Party resource constraints or weak recruitment rather than absolute political security. An unopposed win does not foreclose the possibility of future challenge if economic conditions shift, if education quality declines, or if a credible alternative emerges. Any representative or successor who treats a 100% unopposed result as a signal that continued outreach is unnecessary is vulnerable to a challenger who invests in building relationships in a district where 19.6% are renters and economic anxiety — even in affluent areas — can shift quickly." },
          { name: "Assuming Property-Owning Affluence Means Absence of Economic Concern", tag: "avoid", why: "While MS-HD-74's $94,932 median income and 80.4% homeownership suggest economic security, recent years have demonstrated that affluent suburban constituencies are vulnerable to concerns about healthcare costs, education funding adequacy, property tax burden, and retirement security even at higher income levels. A representative who campaigns or governs as though high income automatically translates to voter satisfaction on economic questions — rather than asking specifically what economic pressures exist within the district's professional class — misses the opportunity to build deeper constituent relationships. Healthcare costs for professional families approaching retirement age, education funding pressures from state budget cuts, and property tax implications of residential development are all real concerns in affluent districts that deserve specific policy engagement." }
