          { name: "Black Plurality Constituent Service", tag: "lean-into", why: "At 32.96% of the population, the Black plurality is large enough to determine the outcome in a contested race, yet the 2023 unopposed victory provided no accountability signal to this constituency around economic mobility, healthcare access, or education quality. A Democratic challenger will target Black voters with specific messages about racial equity, economic opportunity, and government responsiveness—messages an incumbent must answer with proof of service, not assumption of loyalty. Without demonstrated constituent investment in Black community needs, the incumbent cedes the 33% to any credible challenger." },
          { name: "Renter Population and Housing Vulnerability", tag: "careful", why: "The 20.6% renter rate represents roughly 1-in-5 households in economic precarity relative to homeowners, and this constituency is typically underrepresented in partisan turnout but highly responsive to targeted messaging on housing affordability and tenant protections. In a district with modest median income and real economic pressure, a challenger campaign focused on housing costs and rental market fairness can mobilize this group as a swing constituency. An incumbent must address renter concerns directly rather than assuming they follow homeowner preferences." },
          { name: "Treating an Unopposed Victory as Proof of Durability", tag: "avoid", why: "Running unopposed in 2023 indicates an absence of Democratic challenge, not a secure electoral base in a district with 33% Black plurality, 56% white working-class majority, and median income below state average. An unopposed result provides zero data on whether the incumbent is retaining the Black plurality's support or satisfying white working-class economic concerns. A 2025 challenger with resources and a focused message on healthcare access or economic inequality can rapidly redefine the race in a constituency that has not been asked to evaluate the incumbent's performance in a contested environment." },
          { name: "Partisan Messaging Over Constituent Service", tag: "avoid", why: "In a low-income, working-class district where 14.8% college attainment means voters prioritize material household concerns over ideological appeals, campaigns organized around abstract conservative or Republican positioning will underperform against a challenger offering concrete economic solutions. A representative who addresses healthcare affordability, education funding, and workforce development with specificity will hold this seat; one who relies on partisan identity or inherited voter loyalty in a district this economically vulnerable will lose to a credible opponent offering tangible relief." }
