          { name: "Rural Infrastructure & Agricultural Economy Viability", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 16.7% renter rate indicates that roughly one-in-six households lack property ownership, but the remaining ~83% are homeowners or agricultural families whose economic stakes are rooted in rural land and infrastructure. Rural road maintenance, water system reliability, agricultural support services, and the cost of providing rural emergency services are the concrete governance issues that sustain or degrade daily life in this constituency. At 15.1% college education and $55,563 income, this is a district where people depend on functional rural infrastructure and where legislative failure on these issues has immediate, visible economic consequences." },
          { name: "Black Community Engagement & Democratic Vulnerability", tag: "careful", why: "With 21.21% Black population, MS-HD-79 has a substantial African American constituency that appears entirely absent from the current political dynamic — an unopposed Republican victory with no Democratic challenger suggests either that the Black community is effectively disenfranchised from electoral choice or that it is not being engaged by either major party. This represents a significant swing constituency: if Democratic infrastructure rebuilds in the district and puts forward a candidate who speaks to Black constituents' concerns around economic opportunity, healthcare access, and criminal justice, the current 100% Republican margin could shift rapidly. The current situation is not consensus; it is invisibility masking latent electoral competition." },
          { name: "Imposing Culture-War Framing on a Working-Class Economic Constituency", tag: "avoid", why: "MS-HD-79 is a working-class, aging, lower-education district where households are materially focused on healthcare costs, wage adequacy, and rural service survival — not on abstract cultural conflicts. A legislative agenda organized primarily around culture-war positioning rather than concrete responses to economic insecurity will not resonate with this constituency's actual lived experience and will appear disconnected from the material concerns that actually shape their daily lives. This framing gap is exactly where a credible challenger could establish credibility by focusing on what people in this district actually care about." },
          { name: "Neglecting the Black Constituency as a Baseline Assumption", tag: "avoid", why: "An incumbent who represents the district as though it is effectively all-white and organizes constituent service and legislative agenda around that assumption is abandoning 21% of the population and creating political space for an alternative. The unopposed 2023 result does not justify ignoring the Black community as a permanent feature of district politics; if anything, it demonstrates how quickly an organized challenger could mobilize that constituency around economic and healthcare concerns that have been politically invisible. Active Black constituent engagement is both a governance obligation and a basic electoral insurance policy." }
