          { name: "Aging Homeowner Fixed-Cost Management", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 41.8 median age and 77.2% homeownership, MS-HD-81 is a district of aging asset holders with fixed incomes increasingly squeezed by rising property taxes, insurance, maintenance costs, and healthcare expenses. At $54,729 income, many homeowners are not cushioned against cost shocks. Property tax fairness, homestead exemptions, insurance regulation, and local service sustainability are the concrete issues that drive this constituency's voting behavior and are amenable to specific legislative remedies." },
          { name: "Black Community Civic Participation & Turnout", tag: "careful", why: "The 23.43% Black population in an unopposed election is a significant demographic group whose level of organization and turnout is unknown but potentially consequential. If Black voter turnout increases or a credible Democratic challenger emerges with institutional support from the Black community, the seat becomes genuinely competitive; if Black civic participation remains suppressed or unorganized, the unopposed margin persists. Any candidate must understand whether the 23.43% is mobilized or dormant, and structure engagement accordingly." },
          { name: "Treating an Unopposed Win as Evidence of Durable Strength", tag: "avoid", why: "The unopposed result is not evidence that the seat is secure; it is evidence that no organized opposition mounted a challenge in 2023. This is a material difference. In a district with 23.43% Black population, 22.8% renters at $54,729 income, and an aging homeowner base managing fixed-cost pressure, the substrate for a competitive race exists—and any incumbent who interprets an unopposed win as permission to ignore the opposition's potential weaknesses is strategically misreading the district." },
          { name: "Neglecting the Unaccounted-for 3.49% Demographic Gap", tag: "avoid", why: "The 3.49% gap between reported Census categories and 100% population represents real people whose demographic category is either American Indian, multiracial, or other—and who have not been explicitly analyzed as a constituency. In Mississippi's geography, this gap may represent organized tribal or Indigenous community members with specific governance concerns around sovereignty, healthcare access, or economic development; ignoring this population because it is small is a failure of representative governance and a missed opportunity for authentic constituent engagement." }
