        memoParagraphs: [
          "MS-HD-107 is a working-class, majority-white district with material economic pressure points that an unopposed electoral environment has allowed to remain politically invisible. At $57,221 median household income with only 18% college-educated adults, this is a constituency for whom healthcare costs, wage stagnation, housing affordability, and the ability to keep bills paid are the central governing questions — not abstract policy debates. The 37.5-year median age indicates a younger-to-middle-aged workforce population, not a retired or affluent demographic, and the 19% renter rate means that roughly one-fifth of the district is paying housing costs on working-class incomes with no equity stake or asset appreciation. Lott's unopposed 2023 victory with 100% support should not be misread as evidence of consensus or governing satisfaction; it reflects the absence of a challenger, not the presence of voter enthusiasm or alignment. In a competitive election, a candidate who articulates specific answers to working-class economic concerns — healthcare access, wage standards, cost of living — will find real traction with a constituency that has been invisible in an unopposed cycle.",
          "The 11.32% Black population in MS-HD-107 represents both a historically marginalized community and a constituency that an unopposed incumbent has not been required to actively serve or engage. Black households in working-class Mississippi districts face higher unemployment, lower homeownership rates, and greater barriers to healthcare access and small business capital than white households at similar incomes — material inequities that are not addressed by generic economic messaging. The 2.25% Hispanic population, while smaller, faces similar economic barriers and labor-market disadvantages. Any candidate seeking to build a majority coalition or challenge the incumbent must recognize that unopposed elections create invisibility for communities that do not fit the assumed base, and that economic messaging that treats 'working-class concerns' as a white experience will fail to mobilize the 13.5% of the district that is Black or Hispanic. Similarly, an incumbent who wants to retain this seat in a competitive environment must shift from unopposed governance to active, specific engagement with Black and Hispanic constituents on their material economic concerns — not as a charity, but as a recognition that these communities are part of the working-class constituency that defines MS-HD-107."
        ],
