          { name: "Rural Service Delivery & Infrastructure in a Low-Density District", tag: "lean-into", why: "Rural Mississippi districts with 13.8% renter rates and 86% homeownership depend on reliable rural infrastructure—roads, water systems, emergency services, broadband—that becomes more expensive to maintain as populations age and consolidate around county seats. The modest $64,050 income constrains household ability to absorb cost increases in service delivery, and state investment in rural infrastructure is a direct economic security concern for a constituency with fixed assets in dispersed settlements. This is the practical governance issue that determines whether rural homeowners can maintain their assets and remain in place as they age, or whether cost pressures force migration or asset liquidation." },
          { name: "The 3.04% Demographic Gap & Potential AIAN Community", tag: "careful", why: "The four reported Census categories sum to 96.81%, leaving a 3.04% gap that, in rural Mississippi, most likely represents American Indian/Alaska Native residents or recent migration not fully captured in 2020 Census data. While small, this population deserves specific constituent engagement rather than invisibility; if AIAN residents are present, they have governance and service needs (health access, cultural recognition, economic development partnership) that an unopposed incumbent may have deferred. In the next contested election, a challenger who engages this community with respect and specificity could mobilize turnout that the incumbent assumes does not exist." },
          { name: "Assuming Unopposed Victory Indicates Durable Electoral Security", tag: "avoid", why: "A 100% unopposed result in 2023 reflects the absence of a credible challenger, not the absence of potential voter dissatisfaction; treating it as evidence that the current coalition is immovable and that consolidating the existing Republican base is sufficient strategy is arithmetically and strategically wrong. Rural districts with aging homeowner majorities and modest incomes are increasingly vulnerable to challengers who speak specifically to economic pressures (healthcare, property tax, service decline) with credibility, and an unopposed incumbent who has not tested their strength on these issues risks being surprised in the next competitive cycle." },
          { name: "Neglecting the Demographic Gap & Potential Invisible Constituencies", tag: "avoid", why: "The 3.04% gap between reported Census categories and 100% population, though small, represents real people who do not appear in the headline demographic story; neglecting to clarify whether they are AIAN residents, recent migrants, or data variance is a missed opportunity for both governance and electoral intelligence. Even small communities deserve representation and engagement, and ignoring them as a political matter means losing the electoral intelligence about whether they might be mobilized by a future challenger who takes them seriously." }
