          "The 100% result and unopposed status create a false sense of security that is dangerous for any incumbent approaching the next cycle. In a district where 42.8% of adults hold college degrees and median income is $74,311, voters have the economic stability and educational background to evaluate candidates on competence and specific policy rather than inherited partisan loyalty. The 26.2% renter rate signals a meaningful population of younger professionals and mobile households who are less rooted in traditional community networks and more likely to respond to direct candidate messaging. If an organized challenger — whether from within the Republican primary or from the opposing party — identifies specific governance failures, constituent service gaps, or policy disagreements that resonate with this educated, economically secure base, the current 100% margin is not predictive of future electoral safety. The unopposed victory in 2023 represents absence of opposition, not endorsement of performance."
