          "The 2023 unopposed result creates an immediate strategic vulnerability for the incumbent and an opportunity for any challenger willing to invest in this district. In a 53% renter constituency at $63,992 median income, housing affordability, wage growth, and the cost of living are not secondary issues — they are the primary drivers of household financial security. A challenger who campaigns on concrete housing policy (zoning reform, rental regulation, down-payment assistance), wage and labor standards, and the cost of utilities and transportation will find receptive voters regardless of the incumbent's 2023 margin. The fact that Felsher ran unopposed does not indicate voter satisfaction; it indicates the absence of mobilized opposition. The 34.0% college attainment suggests a constituency that is economically literate but not exclusively white-collar — these are workers in skilled trades, service sectors, and mid-level management for whom material economic conditions are the primary measure of representative effectiveness. Any candidate who takes this district seriously must begin by acknowledging that a renter-majority, working-class constituency has fundamentally different economic priorities than a homeowner-majority district, and that housing affordability and wage security are not peripheral issues but central to what representation means in MS-HD-117."
