          "Build credibility on working-class economic security: wages, labor standards, utility costs, childcare affordability, and healthcare access are the issues that matter to a 40.0-year-old, 34% college-educated, $63,992 income constituency; abstract free-market positioning without answers to these concrete problems is unlikely to move voters who are evaluating representatives on whether they are delivering.",
          "Engage the 21.5% Black community and 7.92% Hispanic community with specific attention to their economic mobility and housing security concerns — the district's diversity means that no candidate can win without building genuine relationships with multiple communities, each of which has distinct economic and social priorities within the broader working-class framework.",
          "Recognize that an unopposed 2023 victory is not a mandate or a floor — it is an absence of opposition that creates an opening for any challenger willing to invest in the district and speak directly to housing, wages, and economic security; the incumbent must treat this seat as perpetually competitive rather than safe."
