          "The demographic composition demands careful attention: 20.39% Black residents in a $76,938 median-income district suggests a Black middle-class and professional population whose economic concerns—healthcare access, education quality, property values, and equitable municipal services—differ materially from rural or economically distressed Black constituencies. These voters are not monolithic and not automatically Democratic; suburban Black professionals evaluate on education funding, tax fairness, and local governance competence. Simultaneously, the 33.5% college attainment rate and $76,938 income profile place this district in the upper range for education and economic stability, creating a constituency that prioritizes quality schools, infrastructure maintenance, property tax predictability, and public safety. The unopposed victory means McHenry has not yet had to compete for these voters' attention or persuade them on the specific issues that matter most—and a challenger who enters this race with clarity on what a $76,938-income, college-educated, 20% Black suburban constituency actually needs from local representation can define the race's terms before the incumbent has articulated a defense."
