          { name: "Allowing the Low College Attainment Rate to Become an Electoral Liability Rather Than a Policy Priority", tag: "avoid", why: "The 18.0% college attainment rate signals a workforce with limited post-secondary credentials and reduced access to economic mobility—but treating this as a fixed demographic reality rather than a policy opportunity means conceding the district's economic future. Workforce development, community college affordability and accessibility, and career pathways in specific sectors (healthcare, manufacturing, infrastructure) are not separate from the incumbent's electoral strategy; they are central to demonstrating that government is investing in the constituency's long-term economic security. Ignoring this issue or treating it as secondary to other Democratic messaging will allow Republican opposition to position themselves as focused on economic opportunity in a way that resonates with voters frustrated by stagnant mobility." }
