          { name: "Housing Affordability & Renter Economic Security", tag: "lean-into", why: "The 39.5% renter rate in a district with a $48,519 median household income signals a constituency experiencing housing cost pressure; renters at this income level typically spend 35-50% of gross income on rent, creating fragility around food security, healthcare access, and emergency savings. This is not an abstract housing policy debate — it is the lived economic reality for nearly 2 in 5 households in the district. A candidate who can address renter stabilization, rental assistance, and the relationship between housing costs and wage stagnation speaks directly to a constituency whose material conditions demand political attention and whose turnout patterns may reflect whether they believe either candidate is seriously engaging their economic security." },
          { name: "White Working-Class Economic Base & Wage Trends", tag: "careful", why: "At 65.01% of the district with a median age of 31.3 and median household income of $48,519, white voters in MS-SD-15 are predominantly working-class and younger than statewide averages — a constituency whose economic stability depends on wage growth, local job quality, and cost-of-living management rather than asset appreciation or fixed investment income. The unopposed 2023 result may reflect either solid Republican alignment or insufficient Democratic candidate presence; understanding whether white working-class voters are genuinely satisfied with current economic conditions or simply uncontested is critical to assessing the seat's actual competitiveness. Wage trends, manufacturing employment, and whether young families can afford to remain in the district are the underlying economic facts that determine whether the Republican hold is durable or vulnerable to a credible challenger who addresses these concerns directly." },
          { name: "Treating Unopposed Victory as Evidence of Durability", tag: "avoid", why: "An unopposed 2023 result tells you nothing about how the district would vote in a genuinely contested election. The 0% Democratic vote share is an artifact of candidate recruitment failure, not voter preference; a district with 28.22% Black population, 39.5% renters, and a $48,519 median income has real Democratic constituencies whose absence from the 2023 result reflects absence of candidacy, not absence of potential support. Any incumbent who interprets an unopposed win as evidence of a safe seat is setting themselves up for surprise opposition in a cycle where Democratic recruitment capacity increases." },
          { name: "Assuming College-Educated Voters Are Aligned with Current Leadership", tag: "avoid", why: "At 36.5% college-educated adults, MS-SD-15 has a meaningful educated constituency whose political alignment cannot be assumed based on partisan history alone — particularly in a district where educational attainment is rising and younger adults (median age 31.3) may have different policy priorities than older generations. College-educated voters in economically pressured districts often swing based on specific policy performance rather than inherited party loyalty, and in an unopposed race, their actual preferences are unknowable. A candidate who assumes college-educated voters are safely in their coalition without testing and earning that support through specific policy engagement is missing both a strength and a vulnerability." }
