          "The district's economic baseline reveals the material conditions that determine voter behavior more reliably than partisan history. With a median household income below the state average, renters spending 35-50% of gross income on housing, and 65% white working-class voters with a median age of 31.3, MS-SD-15 is a constituency evaluating government performance on concrete economic measures: whether wages are keeping pace with inflation, whether young families can afford to stay, whether housing is accessible, and whether there are credible pathways to economic mobility for Black and working-class voters. The 36.5% college-educated rate signals a constituency sophisticated enough to shift based on policy performance rather than partisan inheritance. A holding strategy that relies on the incumbent's position without active engagement of the district's material economic concerns—particularly among Black voters, renters, and younger working-class families—is a strategy built on the false confidence of an unopposed race, not on genuine constituent alignment."
