          { name: "Active Constituent Service & Government Navigation", tag: "lean-into", why: "With 20.9% college attainment, a significant portion of MS-SD-16 constituents lack educational credentials that facilitate independent navigation of government services and benefits systems; the representative's office must function as an accessible institution for casework, benefits advocacy, and government problem-solving. At $33,838 income, a family facing eviction, utility shutoff, or healthcare access barriers depends on their representative's office for assistance—and visible, responsive casework is the foundation of durable constituent relationship. Investment in constituent service infrastructure—outpost hours, responsive staff, and visible advocacy—is not peripheral to the representative's work; it is the core relationship that makes the seat genuinely secure." },
          { name: "Latent Primary Challenge Risk", tag: "careful", why: "The unopposed 2023 victory may create a false sense of security that masks vulnerability to a primary challenger who can credibly claim to be delivering more aggressively on economic issues for the low-income constituent base. At 35.2 median age with 45.3% renters and $33,838 income, MS-SD-16 has a constituency that is not ideologically locked into any incumbent—it evaluates on economic delivery, and a primary challenger running on more aggressive advocacy for economic security could contest this seat if the incumbent is perceived as complacent or disconnected from constituent needs. The representative should treat the unopposed victory as an opportunity to deepen constituent engagement and legislative delivery, not as evidence that the seat is secure against organized primary opposition." },
          { name: "Treating the 64.3% Black Democratic Base as Passive", tag: "avoid", why: "The assumption that 64.3% Black population in a Democratic district translates to automatic reelection encourages complacency about actual constituent service, economic delivery, and ongoing relationship-building. African American voters in low-income Mississippi constituencies have demonstrated the capacity to organize alternative candidates and primary challenges when they perceive incumbent neglect or insufficient advocacy for their economic concerns. An unopposed victory is an absence of organized opposition, not evidence that the constituency is satisfied with the incumbent's work; treating it as such creates the conditions for a credible challenger to emerge." },
          { name: "Focusing on Partisan Performance Rather Than Constituent Economic Security", tag: "avoid", why: "A representative who measures success by voting with the party caucus or legislative attendance while remaining distant from constituent economic needs is misaligning political capital with constituent priorities in a district where material economic survival is the organizing concern. Constituents in MS-SD-16 care whether their representative is fighting for Medicaid expansion, wage advocacy, and renter protections—not whether they attend party meetings or speak to partisan messaging. A legislative agenda driven by partisan positioning rather than active economic advocacy for low-income families and workers is a pathway to primary vulnerability and eroded constituent trust." }
